WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:06.680
CCG and Direct Commissioning Assurance Processes
Barbara Hakin

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.040
Chief Operating Officer
Preamble

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:09.070
Thank you, Chairman.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:14.570
This paper and the two attached frameworks
are here to seek the Board's approval to formally

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:21.039
publish the frameworks on CCG and our own
direct commissioning activities and the assurance

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:22.039
of them.

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:29.349
There has been very significant engagement
about these documents with CCGs and our direct

00:00:29.349 --> 00:00:34.250
commissioning units, and all the stakeholders
relevant to our commissioning, since we published

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.000
the CCG interim assurance framework in May.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.280
Frameworks
Domains

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:47.269
I think that there are two or three important
aspects of the two frameworks that they both

00:00:47.269 --> 00:00:48.670
have in common.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:55.359
The first one is that they are both underpinned
by the domains that we used in authorisation

00:00:55.359 --> 00:01:00.670
to be the hallmarks of a good commissioning
organisation.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:07.450
The authorisation process I think was widely
acclaimed as successful and appropriate, and

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:10.080
it is good to see that those domains have
stood the test of time.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.650
If I can just remind the Board -- because
I do think they are so important -- the first

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:18.820
domain is that this is an organisation with
quality and clinical engagement absolutely

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:25.240
at the heart of what it does, and improving
quality for patients is the fundamental essence.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:31.890
The second domain is making sure that patients
and the public are engaged in all the activities,

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:40.360
not only in designing services for communities,
but individual patients having a way of ensuring

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:43.390
that there are services for them, that they
have real choice.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:50.270
That it is predicated on sound plans that
will deliver specific outcomes improvements.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:56.140
That the organisation is well governed, that
the governance arrangements are robust.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:02:03.229
That the organisation works in partnership
will all those others where they need to deliver

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:07.490
improvements for patients, and that they have
confidence in the leadership of the organisation.

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:12.880
That is the first thing about the frameworks.

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.040
CCG assurance framework
The second is that we have changed the nature

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.600
of the CCG assurance framework since the interim
publication in May, to make it much more of

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:30.580
a qualitative, summative conversation arrangement
between our area teams -- who obviously have

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:36.950
responsibility for that and the CCGs -- rather
than the initial one, which started to look

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:41.550
a bit more like a slightly old-style performance
management framework.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.820
That is not to say that this is not any more
robust in any shape or form, and it is absolutely

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:52.380
key and critical that, party to that in the
dashboard, we do have, or we look at on a

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:53.380
quarterly basis.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.870
all those things that CCGs absolutely have
to deliver for the public, whether they are

00:02:56.870 --> 00:03:01.490
in the constitution or they are other issues
where we have made promises.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:07.440
Where it has moved to, it is much more to
tailored conversations for individual CCGs

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.560
based on their circumstances; it is much more
of a responsibility for the CCGs to demonstrate

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:19.870
to us why they can demonstrate that they are
making a difference for patients, and how

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.160
they are doing it.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:27.220
Again, all of that is backed up by real, hard-edged
evidence from 360-degree views, from talking

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:31.650
with all the other stakeholders, from making
sure that they have the right documentation

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:36.340
in place that they can submit to us that shows
that they really are delivering all the things

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.680
that we have asked from the six domains.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.410
Then threading through it all is something
very much about the behaviours and the way

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:49.380
that support, development and assurance are
two sides of a coin, so that we make sure

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:55.550
that this process is much more about helping,
supporting and developing CCGs to be the best

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:59.650
they can be, rather than simply marking their
homework through assurance.

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:05.040
However, the information that we can give
them about how we see them and we reflect

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.890
that to them will be key to their development.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:10.150
Direct commissioning framework
If we move on to the direct commissioning

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.830
framework, it is necessarily different for
two reasons.

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:17.439
One, because we did not have authorisation
for our direct commissioning, so we do not

00:04:17.439 --> 00:04:20.379
have that baseline of evidence.

00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:25.080
So in its early phases, the direct commissioning
assurance will need to do the sort of baseline

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.669
we did for authorisation.

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:32.460
Also, of course, they are not whole organisations,
so at the moment, what we are proposing here

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:41.300
is that we ask our regional offices to oversee
the assurance of the area team units that

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:48.020
commission the direct commissioning, and to
run that process for us on behalf of NHS England.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:54.289
Obviously, again, this will be work in progress
as we start to understand better how they

00:04:54.289 --> 00:04:55.289
can do that.

00:04:55.289 --> 00:04:59.820
There are perhaps a couple of questions that
I might leave the Board with on the two frameworks,

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:02.070
particularly on the direct commissioning.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:10.150
For the CCGs, it is clear that the CCGs will
publish their full assurance results for their

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:12.385
patients and the public at the end of the
year when they have them.

00:05:12.385 --> 00:05:15.099
It will be clear for everyone to see and it
will be clear to the Health and Wellbeing

00:05:15.099 --> 00:05:19.611
Boards exactly how they are doing, what the
full, balanced scorecard looks like, and their

00:05:19.611 --> 00:05:23.139
action plan.

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:24.490
I think we need to start to think.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:32.040
We have not absolutely finalised what we will
do in terms of the direct commissioning, so

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.759
we need to think about that and we need to
think about whether there is a way we can

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:38.669
do that to and through the Health and Wellbeing
Boards.

00:05:38.669 --> 00:05:44.759
I think the second question about the direct
commissioning is whether we then need to start

00:05:44.759 --> 00:05:47.440
to think about how we look at ourselves as
a whole organisation.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:53.720
Clearly, we are looking here at the ability
of the area teams, but they are hugely dependent

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:57.830
on all sorts of other things that go on in
the regions, in the national support centres,

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:02.270
in and around this table, in the Board, and
in the way we govern the organisations, which

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:07.949
we have looked at for CCGs and which this
particular document does not start to get

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:09.870
into for NHS England.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:11.909
That would be something we would want to look
at.

00:06:11.909 --> 00:06:14.201
Work in progress
As ever with these things, this is a work

00:06:14.201 --> 00:06:16.720
in progress; this is our first year of this.

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:20.250
I am sure there will be lots of learning,
I am sure there will be things that we can

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:24.710
improve on; but we will try and make sure
that the whole process is developmental for

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:25.710
everybody.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.949
I think it will be as much developmental -- particularly
with the CCG one -- for NHS England as the

00:06:29.949 --> 00:06:37.550
assuror as it will be for the CCGs in terms
of being assured.

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:43.039
Q&amp;A
Margaret Casely-Hayford: It strikes me that

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:55.860
the move that has been made from a sort of
performance-management base to actually making

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.650
sure that the CCGs are asking themselves the
right questions -- in other words, that we

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:06.569
are in the right assurance space, that we
are actually allowing them to set the standards,

00:07:06.569 --> 00:07:15.189
monitor themselves and come back to us with
the right assurance responses -- is absolutely

00:07:15.189 --> 00:07:18.180
the right thing, and I am really delighted
to see that.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:25.990
I do think that the way we expect responses
back from the area teams should not be too

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:27.340
dissimilar, to be honest.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:36.740
I think that, given that we should be leading
from the front -- albeit that the areas team

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:44.900
do have to look to other organisations, to
other areas -- it will be necessary for them

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:51.779
to be saying to themselves, 'To the extent
that we have to rely elsewhere, are we asking

00:07:51.779 --> 00:08:00.039
for similar sets of standards, questions to
be asked, so that we can respond in the same

00:08:00.039 --> 00:08:06.629
way,' so that, in other words, for every delegation,
there is an assurance response coming back

00:08:06.629 --> 00:08:09.599
up the line on the other side.

00:08:09.599 --> 00:08:13.960
To the extent that they satisfy themselves,
they can satisfy us.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:19.360
It is not a mirror image of what is going
on within the CCGs, but it is not dissimilar.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:23.319
I do think that is very important.

00:08:23.319 --> 00:08:26.469
Barbara Hakin: Absolutely.

00:08:26.469 --> 00:08:28.830
Tim Kelsey: Just one thing.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:33.380
It was not clear to me how committed we are
to the transparency of the process, which

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:38.110
I think, particularly for us, is very important,
in particular, around primary care services,

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:42.840
where people had got used to public board
meetings and so on.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:46.930
Perhaps it is something that we should discuss
afterwards.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.890
We have a dashboard, we are going to have
some structure conversations.

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:53.560
How much, in your mind, are we making those
structure conversations public?

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:56.191
Are we going to be reporting them?

00:08:56.191 --> 00:09:00.410
Will there be some means by which we can get
access to those?

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:05.620
Barbara Hakin: I think to some extent you
are raising the same question that I did in

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:09.290
that we have not absolutely finalised how
we will do this, but somehow, we have to.

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:17.060
When we were talking about this, David suggested
that the Health and Wellbeing Board has to

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:24.100
be key and kernel to this, so I think we need
to make sure that we report on the different

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.990
aspects of direct commissioning separately.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:30.410
I cannot say too often that the only thing
NHS England's direct commissioning functions

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:36.930
have in common is that NHS England commissions
them, and they are done on different units.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:42.820
I think that every Health and Wellbeing Board
deserves to know for its population across

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:46.470
those five how well those services are being
commissioned.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:50.810
Therefore, that is the basis of our mutual
accountability with others around the table.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:56.240
David Nicholson: I do think this is really
important, and it will become increasingly

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:04.320
important as we move through the development
of the integration fund, going forward.

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.550
Although Health and Wellbeing Boards have
developed in different ways in different places,

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:12.600
they will have some governance responsibilities
in relation to those pooled budgets.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:19.310
With that, I think there comes a much more
open and regularised way of people working,

00:10:19.310 --> 00:10:25.390
and I absolutely see the place where NHS England,
our staff in the area teams, will be accounting

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:29.700
to those people for what they have done with
the money and the decisions that they have

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:30.700
been taking.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:32.751
I think that is a good thing, and if we can
do it openly and transparently and manage

00:10:32.751 --> 00:10:35.840
it that way, I think it will be really quite
positive.

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:41.200
Ed Smith: I will just make the point that
I massively welcome this; I think it is great.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:48.281
I think there has to be or will need to be
a parallel process, because each CCG, when

00:10:48.281 --> 00:10:53.450
they come to the end of their year, will have
to sign, probably at Chair and Audit Committee

00:10:53.450 --> 00:11:00.160
Chair level, a governance statement, which
will come in to us, because David and the

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:06.360
Chairman will need to provide their own governance
statement on the consolidated picture of NHS

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.030
England, including all of the CCGs.

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:13.560
The timing for that, quite frankly, is relatively
soon after their year-end.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:21.370
It will have to be externally audited by their
local auditors, and it is very, very important

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:26.430
that the whole system is geared up to providing
that assurance in addition to what I think

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:29.970
is going to be a later set of assurance around
this.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:36.300
Whilst this is really, really important, I
cannot overstate how important that first

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:40.600
set of governance statements is going to be,
and we have to make sure the system is really

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:46.660
ready for it in advance and there is no inconsistency
between the two sets of statements that come

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:47.660
through the process.

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:51.700
Barbara Hakin: I think that is really vital,
and of course, the authorisation process,

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:57.580
which was done at different stages for different
CCGs, they authorised them on the basis that

00:11:57.580 --> 00:12:03.440
they had a robust governance structure and
were planning to have the systems and processes

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:04.740
in place.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:05.740
I think there are two things.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:10.630
I think, as you have pointed out, we need
to be very clear from CCGs that they put themselves

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:17.200
in a position to assure NHS England's Board
that that structure has continued, that the

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:22.910
proper processes have been followed, and that
with the additional assurance from internal

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:27.640
and external auditors, they can be very clear
to us that they have followed the processes

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:32.260
and can give us assurance about their accounts.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:38.550
I think that, also, NHS England would need
to be vigilant in any soft intelligence or

00:12:38.550 --> 00:12:44.070
any reports from CCGs it picked up, if we
foresaw any problems there, because I think

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:48.030
you are right; I think that looking at their
governance review and the assurance that will

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.750
come too late for this specific issue.

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:53.150
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:59.690
I think that it is absolutely clear that this
Board needs assurance on both, on the CCGs

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:02.030
and on direct commissioning.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:07.490
On the CCGs, we commend the approach that
you are proposing, and that it is an open

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:08.680
and transparent model.

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:15.610
We know that there are always limits to the
assurance process, and the use of soft intelligence

00:13:15.610 --> 00:13:20.200
thus to test the returns that we are getting
will be absolutely critical, I think.

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:28.430
We take on board the Deputy Chairman's caution
to us about time and the need to get on with

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:30.750
this promptly at year-end.

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:37.470
Finally, I think in principle, our own commitment
to ensuring that our assurance of ourselves

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:44.040
and, within us, the direct commissioning processes
and the different systems, is in parallel

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.350
with what we expect from CCGs.

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:52.890
I think this is quite a demanding process
when we are still an organisation that is

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:58.530
settling down and trying to stabilise direct
commissioning, which is not a trivial matter.

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:02.320
Nonetheless, we are only going to function
properly as a Board, I think, if we have a

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:03.550
parallel model of assurance.

